  "answer": {
    "text": "## Answer\n\nI can't confirm a latency spike at 21:08 — the metrics show something different, and I'd be inventing a cause if I explained one.\n\n**What the data actually shows:** the only HTTP traffic in the last ~7 hours occurred between roughly 21:07:30 and 21:12:30. Before and after that, `http_request_duration_highr_seconds_bucket` has no samples at all, so p95 is `NaN` for every step from 15:00 to 21:07 and again from 21:13 onward [prometheus:31686819]. Request rate is flat 0 outside that window for all three handlers (`/items`, `/jobs`, `/jobs/stats`) [prometheus:32220834].\n\nSo the thing that \"starts\" at 21:08 on the dashboard's latency panel is the series existing at all, not a rise from a healthy baseline. There is no pre-21:08 baseline to compare against.\n\n**Within the traffic window, latency was flat to falling, not spiking:**\n- p95 went 0.66s (21:08) → 0.58s → 0.51s → 0.41s (21:12) → 0.61s [prometheus:60576682]\n- p99 at 1-minute resolution oscillated between 0.10s and 1.57s across the whole window with no trend, highest at 21:08:30 and again at 21:11 [prometheus:87809733]\n\n**Nothing in the supporting signals points to a fault:**\n- No 5xx at any point; traffic was ~27–28 req/s of 2xx plus ~0.8–1.1 req/s of 4xx, steady for the entire window [prometheus:25307533]\n- Zero alerts firing or pending, including `HighApiErrorRate` [prometheus:95642421]\n- No error/warn/timeout/exception lines from the API component between 21:04 and 21:14 [loki:73296981]\n- Loki and promtail were both fully ready across 21:00–21:55, so the log gap is a real absence, not dropped ingestion [prometheus:69565748]\n\n**Caveat:** my broad log pull for 21:03–21:16 came back `truncated` — it was flooded by `day2-worker` job claim/complete lines and I only saw the most recent 100 [loki:25789450]. The targeted API error search that returned nothing was not truncated [loki:73296981], so the \"no API errors\" conclusion holds; but I have not read every worker or postgres line in that window.\n\n**What this most likely is** — and I'm flagging this as inference, not observation: a ~5-minute burst of synthetic or load-generator traffic against an otherwise idle API, where sub-second p95 is simply the service's normal cost under load. If you want to establish whether ~0.5s p95 is abnormal for these handlers, that needs a comparison against a window with real sustained traffic, and no such window exists in the retained data. Tell me what generated the 21:08 traffic and I can look at the worker and postgres logs for that specific window instead.",
